  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Great outdoor landscape lighting ideas share a few traits: they put light on something worth seeing, they hide the fixture, and they use warm 2700K color so stone and foliage look natural. The ideas below are organized by area of the property, from the front facade to the back fence, with the technique, fixture type, and placement that make each one work. Most are drawn from projects Austin Landscape Lighting has completed across Central Texas, where live oaks, limestone, Hill Country slopes, and long warm evenings shape what looks good. Pick the ideas that fit your yard, and remember that a dozen deliberate fixtures almost always beat two dozen scattered ones.</p>

<h2>Facade and Entry Ideas</h2>
<h3>1. Graze the stone</h3>
<p>Austin stone and chopped limestone have deep texture that disappears at night. Set a brass well light or shrouded uplight 8 to 12 inches from the base of the wall, aimed straight up with a 45 to 60 degree spread, and every joint and face casts a shadow. Space fixtures 6 to 8 feet apart along the wall for even coverage.</p>
<h3>2. Frame the entry with column uplights</h3>
<p>A 15 degree spot at the base of each porch column draws the eye to the front door. Keep the beam tight so it stays on the column and does not spill onto the ceiling.</p>
<h3>3. Downlight the porch ceiling</h3>
<p>Small recessed or surface-mounted downlights in the porch ceiling wash the entry door and welcome mat without glare. Dim them to 50 percent so they do not compete with the facade.</p>
<h3>4. Highlight gables and dormers</h3>
<p>A narrow 15 degree spot aimed at a gable peak or dormer adds height to a single-story home and creates a focal point above the roofline. Shield it carefully so no light escapes above the roof.</p>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/outdoor-landscape-lighting-ideas-2.webp" alt="Grazed limestone facade with column uplights framing the front door of an Austin home at night" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Grazing the limestone from 8 to 12 inches out and spotting the porch columns creates a layered facade with no visible fixtures.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Path and Walkway Ideas</h2>
<h3>5. Stagger path lights</h3>
<p>Place 18 to 22 inch brass path lights every 6 to 8 feet, alternating sides of the walk, 12 to 18 inches back from the edge. Pools of light should just touch. This is the opposite of the runway effect.</p>
<h3>6. Light the walk from above</h3>
<p>Where a walkway passes under a live oak or a pergola, a downlight mounted 10 to 20 feet up replaces several path lights with a single soft pool. Fewer fixtures in the beds, less to trim around.</p>
<h3>7. Use bollards on modern homes</h3>
<p>Low cylindrical bollards with downward louvers suit contemporary architecture better than traditional hat-style path lights. Space them wider, 8 to 10 feet, since they cast a broader pool.</p>
<h3>8. Mark the driveway edge</h3>
<p>Short, sturdy path lights or recessed in-grade markers along a long Hill Country driveway guide guests and protect the lawn edge from tires. Keep them low and shielded for dark-sky compliance.</p>

<h2>Tree Ideas</h2>
<h3>9. Cross-light a live oak</h3>
<p>Multi-trunk live oaks need two or three uplights at the drip line edge, each with a 30 to 38 degree flood at 400 to 900 lumens, aimed from different angles. One beam flattens the tree; three reveal it.</p>
<h3>10. Spot a cedar elm or cypress</h3>
<p>Tall, narrow trees want a single 15 degree spot placed 2 to 3 feet from the trunk. The beam climbs 30 feet or more and lights the canopy from below.</p>
<h3>11. Moonlight from the canopy</h3>
<p>Mount two or three fixtures 20 to 30 feet up in a mature oak or pecan, aimed down through the branches with a 60 degree spread. The dappled shadow pattern on the lawn is the most natural effect in landscape lighting and the best ambient layer for a yard.</p>
<h3>12. Silhouette a specimen against a wall</h3>
<p>Place a fixture behind a yucca, agave, or Japanese maple and aim it at the wall. The plant becomes a dark sculpture against a warm glow.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/landscape-lighting-ideas-for-trees/">landscape lighting ideas for trees</a> post goes deeper on each species common to Austin.</p>

<h2>Patio and Outdoor Living Ideas</h2>
<h3>13. Downlight the dining table</h3>
<p>A fixture in a pergola beam or nearby tree aimed at the table gives enough light to eat by without a glaring overhead. Dim to taste with an app-controlled zone.</p>
<h3>14. Light the grill and counters</h3>
<p>Task lighting under an outdoor kitchen counter overhang or a small downlight above the grill makes cooking safe after dark. Keep it on its own zone so it can go off when dinner is done.</p>
<h3>15. Wash a privacy hedge</h3>
<p>A 60 degree flood 4 to 6 feet from a hedge or fence creates a lit backdrop that makes the patio feel enclosed and larger at the same time.</p>
<h3>16. Underlight built-in seating</h3>
<p>LED strip or small hardscape lights tucked under the cap of a stone bench or fire pit wall float the seating and light the floor without any visible source.</p>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/best-outdoor-landscape-lighting-ideas-2.webp" alt="Austin patio at night with a downlit dining table, washed hedge backdrop, and underlit stone seating wall" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>A patio layered with a downlit table, a washed hedge backdrop, and underlit seating reads as a finished outdoor room.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Pool and Water Feature Ideas</h2>
<h3>17. Light the pool surround, not just the water</h3>
<p>Submersible pool lights do their job, but the surrounding landscape needs its own layer. Uplit palms or oaks behind the pool reflect in the water and double the effect.</p>
<h3>18. Underwater fountain and spillway lights</h3>
<p>Small submersible 12V fixtures at the base of a spillway or fountain make moving water sparkle. Aim them up through the stream, never at the viewer.</p>
<h3>19. Hardscape lights on the coping</h3>
<p>Recessed lights under the pool coping edge or along a raised spa wall mark the water's edge for safety and add a continuous warm line.</p>
<h3>20. Moonlight over the pool</h3>
<p>Downlighting from a nearby tree creates soft, reflected light on the surface and avoids the harshness of pool lights alone. Details are in our <a href="/landscape-lighting-ideas-for-pools/">landscape lighting ideas for pools</a> guide.</p>

<h2>Steps, Walls, and Slope Ideas</h2>
<p>Hill Country grade changes make step and wall lighting a safety requirement, and the table below summarizes the fixture choices that work best for each situation.</p>
