<h2>Garden Bed and Plant Ideas</h2>
<h3>21. Uplight ornamental grasses</h3>
<p>Gulf muhly, Mexican feather grass, and Lindheimer muhly glow when uplit from the base with a 36 degree spread. The seed heads catch light and move in the breeze.</p>
<h3>22. Backlight agave and yucca</h3>
<p>Architectural desert plants common in Austin xeriscapes look best silhouetted or backlit so their sharp outlines show. A fixture placed behind and slightly to one side does it.</p>
<h3>23. Soft wash on foundation plantings</h3>
<p>A low 60 degree wash across dwarf yaupon or Texas sage keeps the base of the house from reading as a dark band beneath a lit facade. Keep the lumen level low so the facade stays the hero.</p>
<h3>24. Light a garden sculpture or boulder</h3>
<p>A single 30 degree uplight on a limestone boulder or a piece of garden art gives the back corner of a yard a destination.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscaping-lights-ideas-2.webp" alt="Uplit ornamental grasses and silhouetted agave in an Austin xeriscape garden bed at night" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Uplit muhly grass and a backlit agave turn a xeriscape bed into a nighttime feature with just two fixtures.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Seasonal and Occasional Ideas</h2>
<h3>25. Add color-changing lamps for holidays</h3>
<p>RGBW lamps in existing brass fixtures let you switch the facade to orange in October, red and green in December, and burnt orange for a game day, then back to 2700K warm white the rest of the year. No extra fixtures or string lights.</p>
<h3>26. Light the fall color</h3>
<p>Austin's cedar elms, Texas red oaks, and Mexican sycamores turn in November. An uplight that is modest in summer becomes spectacular for a few weeks when the leaves change.</p>
<h3>27. Create a summer night garden</h3>
<p>Night-blooming plants such as moonflower, four o'clocks, and white-flowered datura show up beautifully under soft moonlighting. Plan a bed near the patio specifically for after-dark viewing during the long warm season.</p>

<h2>Smart Scene Ideas</h2>
<h3>28. Set an arrival scene</h3>
<p>Program the front facade, path, and driveway to full brightness from sunset until 11 p.m., then drop the facade to 30 percent and leave the path on until midnight. An astronomical timer or app controller adjusts the sunset time automatically through the year.</p>
<h3>29. Build an entertaining scene</h3>
<p>Patio, pool, and back trees at 100 percent, front yard at 50 percent, dining table downlight dimmed to 40 percent. One tap when guests arrive.</p>
<h3>30. Run a late-night security level</h3>
<p>After midnight, dim everything to 20 percent except the path to the trash cans and a soft wash on the garage. Enough to see and deter, low enough for dark-sky and HOA compliance. Our <a href="/services/smart-lighting-systems/">smart lighting systems</a> make these scenes simple to program and change.</p>

<h2>Principles That Tie the Ideas Together</h2>
<p>Whichever ideas you choose, a few constants keep the result cohesive. Use 2700K warm white throughout. Choose solid brass or copper fixtures that survive Austin heat and deer. Hide every source with shrouds, louvers, and smart placement. Build a hierarchy with one hero element and progressively quieter layers. Size the transformer with 20 percent headroom and use hub wiring to avoid voltage drop on long Hill Country runs. Shield fixtures so no light escapes above horizontal. And test at night before burying cable, because every idea above looks different in the dark than on paper.</p>

